[{:db/id "q-339693/field-1/47a8330d52ed42dbfa366c35cf03c2989bfb58abdb997b2d6a304faf2b1255f6" :answer/id #uuid "fc753ea7-a417-56c1-99b8-5ea5adb32c79" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3.55" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339693/field-1/4fdb062a11b0de1705813076f753d12ba89cb94fdf7497dc43fb6340ae032379" :answer/id #uuid "b7eb3536-4b06-599a-8207-8aea091cca69" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-0.32" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339693/field-1/909930bebd0471aaf20a341ddcc13d3017d1cf6a52c3019574196a3def636dee" :answer/id #uuid "a0b1595f-6f6c-5a49-a166-ffd91506ea34" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.32" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339693/field-1/aac73a204fe5ad92f137663aa3208269d1c476f8080b38f340f6f5c69e9173e4" :answer/id #uuid "bef51703-4788-5d65-b4f0-daf828fb5737" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-0.67" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339693/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "493d66e0-1ff0-5516-916f-224a16122077" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-339693/field-1/aac73a204fe5ad92f137663aa3208269d1c476f8080b38f340f6f5c69e9173e4" :answer-field/choices ["q-339693/field-1/47a8330d52ed42dbfa366c35cf03c2989bfb58abdb997b2d6a304faf2b1255f6" "q-339693/field-1/4fdb062a11b0de1705813076f753d12ba89cb94fdf7497dc43fb6340ae032379" "q-339693/field-1/909930bebd0471aaf20a341ddcc13d3017d1cf6a52c3019574196a3def636dee" "q-339693/field-1/aac73a204fe5ad92f137663aa3208269d1c476f8080b38f340f6f5c69e9173e4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-339693" :question/id #uuid "32118057-b660-5919-beb6-d21b2129e10b" :question/math-academy-id "q-339693" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=3xy+y,\\,y(0)=2\n$$\n\nUse the **implicit** Euler method with one step to approximate $y(1)$ rounded to $2$ decimal places.$\\,y(1)≈$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=1,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=1-0=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the implicit Euler method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(3{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}}+{y}_{\\text{new}})\\cdot 1 \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{new}}(3{x}_{\\text{new}}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+{y}_{\\text{new}}(3{x}_{\\text{new}}+1),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}}-{y}_{\\text{new}}(3{x}_{\\text{new}}+1) & =y \\\\ (1-(3{x}_{\\text{new}}+1)){y}_{\\text{new}} & =y \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{y}{1-(3{x}_{\\text{new}}+1)} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{y}{3{x}_{\\text{new}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=0+1=1.$ Hence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{y}{3{x}_{\\text{new}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & ≈-0.67,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(1)≈-0.67.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-339693/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "246a84b2-e9fa-58e5-858c-66c09e1c3bc0"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-339693"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-339694/field-1/bba3868abfbca2614b92d49ab7301c71c3eb3f7d7af71e82858ec477821614f9" :answer/id #uuid "6e1de171-b1bf-5da3-91c2-59dac60ce9ad" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2.51" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339694/field-1/cf04b10b6da6bdf4a35485314a6406ffe8c8dd453e0de34651ae8b36125c5455" :answer/id #uuid "b9df343f-2ef2-5db1-9dd0-79862653687e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-0.31" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339694/field-1/311ec029cae205f3d47f7d1b08933ca6a32e19dfe704995fcd682f2f274f42db" :answer/id #uuid "9ee3b6cf-6e2d-5fc7-86f2-5aabeef98e64" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1.52" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339694/field-1/5042a0651ef29f53af4cf52ea2d8dda9d3c6af1ec8fab9808c8a91b5fd3a237f" :answer/id #uuid "71be9883-f6d8-537e-9ec9-8f0fcff420be" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.39" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339694/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "471ff64a-598d-577d-8084-10f3399448ee" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-339694/field-1/cf04b10b6da6bdf4a35485314a6406ffe8c8dd453e0de34651ae8b36125c5455" :answer-field/choices ["q-339694/field-1/bba3868abfbca2614b92d49ab7301c71c3eb3f7d7af71e82858ec477821614f9" "q-339694/field-1/cf04b10b6da6bdf4a35485314a6406ffe8c8dd453e0de34651ae8b36125c5455" "q-339694/field-1/311ec029cae205f3d47f7d1b08933ca6a32e19dfe704995fcd682f2f274f42db" "q-339694/field-1/5042a0651ef29f53af4cf52ea2d8dda9d3c6af1ec8fab9808c8a91b5fd3a237f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-339694" :question/id #uuid "02054168-89d5-599b-a2c5-093e7487797c" :question/math-academy-id "q-339694" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=y(3x-2),\\,y(1)=4\n$$\n\nUse the **implicit** Euler method with one step to approximate $y(3)$ rounded to $2$ decimal places.$\\,y(3)≈$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=3,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=3-1=2.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the implicit Euler method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{new}}\\cdot (3{x}_{\\text{new}}-2)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2{y}_{\\text{new}}(3{x}_{\\text{new}}-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+2{y}_{\\text{new}}(3{x}_{\\text{new}}-2),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}}-2{y}_{\\text{new}}(3{x}_{\\text{new}}-2) & =y \\\\ (1-2(3{x}_{\\text{new}}-2)){y}_{\\text{new}} & =y \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{y}{(1-2(3{x}_{\\text{new}}-2))} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{y}{5-6{x}_{\\text{new}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=1+2=3.$ Hence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{y}{5-6{x}_{\\text{new}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5-6\\cdot 3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{13} \\\\  & ≈-0.31,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(3)≈-0.31.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-339694/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "246a84b2-e9fa-58e5-858c-66c09e1c3bc0"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-339694"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-339715/field-1/859b68c5cdc3dd58dae0e21219013b55bc93cdcde974df02b7fad26f35842f3e" :answer/id #uuid "8e8dadfa-6c2d-5116-923b-fa1b672b8321" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-0.60" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339715/field-1/9a919a5dd55ee179d5a939b66417540c77a1da1b49a565b4a9ba049471fc60c4" :answer/id #uuid "95dab003-692d-5a4b-8807-d3c870498b6c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.64" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339715/field-1/adad4c0f184f7458e4a49b6a8eaed85e6af578c7d9c4fd39c2e43ca9a038fde7" :answer/id #uuid "4f07a998-d669-5070-b19b-5f805a97c778" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1.32" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339715/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "70fa372c-d1c1-5975-b9c3-d3535edbd7c2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-339715/field-1/859b68c5cdc3dd58dae0e21219013b55bc93cdcde974df02b7fad26f35842f3e" :answer-field/choices ["q-339715/field-1/859b68c5cdc3dd58dae0e21219013b55bc93cdcde974df02b7fad26f35842f3e" "q-339715/field-1/9a919a5dd55ee179d5a939b66417540c77a1da1b49a565b4a9ba049471fc60c4" "q-339715/field-1/adad4c0f184f7458e4a49b6a8eaed85e6af578c7d9c4fd39c2e43ca9a038fde7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-339715/field-2/7ade42e6474e345d72167684e29b24ee59ab680e6b7494ddb6fffdd4ce6b83a8" :answer/id #uuid "dc97684c-2620-5596-8bf1-aff36cee9d13" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1.23" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339715/field-2/13b5694d2972058414e339472820b3e7fb5d0a5ca42aa52a7e10b3ee5b426c91" :answer/id #uuid "ac5d83c0-f762-53f1-9218-833192cdca5f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-0.64" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339715/field-2/78cb8a30475920ae9b315483211256a67c3134650b52b439653a574d3bcc5861" :answer/id #uuid "5f1fdbc5-d9f9-5e1f-938e-61858a9b708c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.46" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339715/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "0c41a988-085f-5def-b21e-266224629790" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-339715/field-2/13b5694d2972058414e339472820b3e7fb5d0a5ca42aa52a7e10b3ee5b426c91" :answer-field/choices ["q-339715/field-2/7ade42e6474e345d72167684e29b24ee59ab680e6b7494ddb6fffdd4ce6b83a8" "q-339715/field-2/13b5694d2972058414e339472820b3e7fb5d0a5ca42aa52a7e10b3ee5b426c91" "q-339715/field-2/78cb8a30475920ae9b315483211256a67c3134650b52b439653a574d3bcc5861"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-339715" :question/id #uuid "1c34c65e-a8c0-51b6-b576-1c0ce071aeca" :question/math-academy-id "q-339715" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-2x-3y,\\,y(0)=-1\n$$\n\nUse the **implicit** Euler method with two steps of equal size to approximate $y(\\frac{1}{2})$ and $y(1)$ rounded to $2$ decimal places.$\\,y(\\frac{1}{2})≈$ {{field-1}},\n$\\,y(1)≈$ {{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y(1).$ The distance from $x=0$ to $x=1$ is $1,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{1-0}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the implicit Euler method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(-2{x}_{\\text{new}}-3{y}_{\\text{new}})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-{x}_{\\text{new}}-\\frac{3}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y-{x}_{\\text{new}}-\\frac{3}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y-{x}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{2(y-{x}_{\\text{new}})}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to calculate our approximations of $y(\\frac{1}{2})$ and $y(1).$\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nFrom the initial conditions, we have\n\n$$\nx=0,\\,y=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=0+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{2(y-{x}_{\\text{new}})}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(-1-\\frac{1}{2})}{5} \\\\  & =-0.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nFrom the previous step, we have\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2},\\,y=-0.6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{2(y-{x}_{\\text{new}})}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(-0.6-1)}{5} \\\\  & =-0.64\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that \n\n$$\ny(\\frac{1}{2})≈-0.60,\\,y(1)≈-0.64.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-339715/field-1" "q-339715/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "56e30ebf-9265-5ba8-888d-6956206b542e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-339715"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-339721/field-1/2794762e4ddfdde0b82fa0fa698238d120e9ffbdf9988f8cca231cf8e524b916" :answer/id #uuid "46a37d9d-19bc-5c83-baba-1f1c47491d05" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3.65" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339721/field-1/a31aaaa6c9f8e916865b360d3915fcf0bd4a2e9f9703b656a75278098bcc5314" :answer/id #uuid "28ac9ec4-ef2b-5977-90a1-cb28718b0079" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1.73" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339721/field-1/9b544d835617b16074f484599e940052dc9e3df611c67aeb7e1a415da4492a0d" :answer/id #uuid "9e6f58f8-b191-5ac3-83fd-a7c8ed6282c7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2.00" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339721/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "1a6d13c7-7e47-5f91-8965-3d05c4467012" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-339721/field-1/2794762e4ddfdde0b82fa0fa698238d120e9ffbdf9988f8cca231cf8e524b916" :answer-field/choices ["q-339721/field-1/2794762e4ddfdde0b82fa0fa698238d120e9ffbdf9988f8cca231cf8e524b916" "q-339721/field-1/a31aaaa6c9f8e916865b360d3915fcf0bd4a2e9f9703b656a75278098bcc5314" "q-339721/field-1/9b544d835617b16074f484599e940052dc9e3df611c67aeb7e1a415da4492a0d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-339721/field-2/2794762e4ddfdde0b82fa0fa698238d120e9ffbdf9988f8cca231cf8e524b916" :answer/id #uuid "c24dbedf-03ac-54ba-9467-894372b09591" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3.65" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339721/field-2/7658f3dd72dadda6c7333aadd57d2b2360af5af4e463e55805661125099bcf17" :answer/id #uuid "172c2adc-a928-5b12-a03d-16ffc437ad92" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9.33" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339721/field-2/a41dba29ffada5acc1fe80c7356f0b4115d22bd6e34dd2d566cadf8e3c6b73a4" :answer/id #uuid "203a3d38-714f-505e-8f67-47b60346ed70" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10.02" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339721/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "bd6273b0-df2c-5ea6-b542-466388f20762" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-339721/field-2/a41dba29ffada5acc1fe80c7356f0b4115d22bd6e34dd2d566cadf8e3c6b73a4" :answer-field/choices ["q-339721/field-2/2794762e4ddfdde0b82fa0fa698238d120e9ffbdf9988f8cca231cf8e524b916" "q-339721/field-2/7658f3dd72dadda6c7333aadd57d2b2360af5af4e463e55805661125099bcf17" "q-339721/field-2/a41dba29ffada5acc1fe80c7356f0b4115d22bd6e34dd2d566cadf8e3c6b73a4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-339721" :question/id #uuid "c3f26762-2007-5a70-9751-caae5ca04520" :question/math-academy-id "q-339721" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={e}^{x}+y,\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nUse the **implicit** Euler method with two steps of equal size to approximate $y(\\frac{1}{2})$ and $y(1)$ rounded to $2$ decimal places.$\\,y(\\frac{1}{2})≈$ {{field-1}},\n$\\,y(1)≈$ {{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y(1).$ The distance from $x=0$ to $x=1$ is $1,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{1-0}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the implicit Euler method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =({e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}+{y}_{\\text{new}})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =2y+{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to calculate our approximations of $y(\\frac{1}{2})$ and $y(1).$\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nFrom the initial conditions, we have\n\n$$\nx=0,\\,y=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=0+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =2y+{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1+{e}^{1/2} \\\\  & ≈3.648\\,721,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $6$ decimal places.\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nFrom the previous step, we have\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2},\\,y≈3.648\\,721.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =2y+{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}} \\\\  & ≈2\\cdot 3.648\\,721+{e}^{1} \\\\  & ≈10.015\\,724,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $6$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, rounded to $2$ decimal places, we conclude that \n\n$$\ny(\\frac{1}{2})≈3.65,\\,y(1)≈10.02.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-339721/field-1" "q-339721/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "56e30ebf-9265-5ba8-888d-6956206b542e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-339721"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186220681 :answer-field/correct 17592186220684 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186220677 :answer-field/correct 17592186220679 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-339722"] :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={e}^{x}+{x}^{2}+y,\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nUse the **implicit** Euler method with two steps of equal size to approximate $y(\\frac{1}{2})$ and $y(1)$ rounded to $2$ decimal places.$\\,y(\\frac{1}{2})≈$ {{field-1}},\n$\\,y(1)≈$ {{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y(1).$ The distance from $x=0$ to $x=1$ is $1,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{1-0}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the implicit Euler method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =({e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}+{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}+{y}_{\\text{new}})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}+{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =2y+{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}+{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to calculate our approximations of $y(\\frac{1}{2})$ and $y(1).$\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nFrom the initial conditions, we have\n\n$$\nx=0,\\,y=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=0+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =2y+{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}+{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1+{e}^{1/2}+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & ≈3.898\\,721\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $6$ decimal places.\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nFrom the previous step, we have\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2},\\,y≈3.898\\,721.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =2y+{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}+{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2} \\\\  & ≈2(3.898\\,721)+{e}^{1}+{1}^{2} \\\\  & ≈11.515\\,724\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $6$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, rounded to $2$ decimal places, we conclude that \n\n$$\ny(\\frac{1}{2})≈3.90,\\,y(1)≈11.52.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
